#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162825} :activity/id #uuid "ccbd1570-797c-5564-8927-e502aa05caec" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050527} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162750 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163179 :tutorial/content "We can do the same trick using the **elementary column operations** instead of row operations.\n\nThere are three types of elementary *column* operations, which are entirely analogous to the corresponding row operations:\n\n- Switching any two columns: \n\n$$\nC_i \\leftrightarrow C_j\n$$\n\n- Multiplying (each element of) a column by a non-zero number: \n\n$$\nC_i := k C_i,\\qquad k \\neq 0\n$$\n\n- Adding a multiple of one column to another column: \n\n$$\nC_j := C_j+k C_i,\\qquad j \\neq i\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** \n\n- Unlike elementary row operations, elementary *column* operations change the solutions of a system of linear equations. This is because column operations alter the coefficients associated with each variable across the system, changing the equations.\n\n- The exception is swapping two columns - this doesn't alter the solutions *provided that* we swap the corresponding variables in the solution vector. For example, if we swap the first and second columns, we must treat the second variable as the first and vice versa. The solution set remains unchanged because the relationships between the variables and their coefficients are preserved." :tutorial/id #uuid "e947134c-e909-5a3b-85a6-d284e04816e0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10652 :tutorial/title "Column Operations"} :step/id #uuid "ce002306-ea12-5d28-8c26-a1710d2f4327" :step/math-academy-id 218771 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162829}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162825 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163214 :tutorial/content "When studying linear algebra, it is common to use elementary row operations to reduce a matrix $A$ to another matrix $B$ such that $B$ contains many zeros. \n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -5 & -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the entry $a_{11}$ located in the top left-hand corner. We can reduce *all* of the entries *below* it using elementary row operations.\n\nFirst, we subtract $3$ times the first row from the second row.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A= & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -5 & -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}: &={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 10 \\\\ -5 & -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we add $5$ times the first row to the third row.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 10 \\\\ -5 & -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}: &={R}_{3}+5{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -14\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we're done! We have successfully performed elementary row operations to reduce all of the entries below $a_{11}$ to zero." :tutorial/id #uuid "1e0cae18-aa8d-5b1d-9aab-4a5334dec0c0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4828 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "b68078fb-1628-5a76-bb97-1c1261f6641d" :step/math-academy-id 30052 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162828}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162826 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161965 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161757 :question/id #uuid "70991204-370a-5804-a04e-7e664db052de" :question/math-academy-id "e-8048" :question/problem "\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ {\\color{blue}1} & \\bbox[lightgray,2pt]{{\\color{blue}1}} & {\\color{blue}2} & {\\color{blue}-1} \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & {\\color{red}0} & -3 & 7 \\\\ {\\color{blue}1} & \\bbox[lightgray,2pt]{{\\color{blue}1}} & {\\color{blue}2} & {\\color{blue}-1} \\\\ -4 & {\\color{red}0} & -5 & 4 \\\\ 2 & {\\color{red}0} & 5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $B$ above. By applying three matrix elementary row operations \n\n- $R_1 := \\ldots$\n\n- $R_3:=R_3-3R_2$\n\n- $R_4:= \\ldots$\n\neach of the form $R_i:=R_i+kR_2,$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the missing elementary row operations?" :question/worked-solution "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make entries above and below $a_{22}={\\color{blue}\\bbox[lightgray,2pt]{1}}$ equal to ${\\color{red}0}.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary row operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}: &={R}_{1}-2{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & -3 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}: &={R}_{3}-3{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & -3 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 0 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{4}: &={R}_{4}+2{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & -3 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 0 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 5 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing elementary row operations are\n\n$\\qquad$ $R_1 := R_1 - 2R_2\\quad$ and $\\quad R_4:=R_4+2R_2.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "21f1ec99-e166-5986-a07d-f07be0dfeb31" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162554} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162571} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265911} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265912} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329695} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357504} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357505} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400478} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402841} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404530} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186640039}] :knowledge-point/title "Using Row Operations to Create a Column of Zeros in a 4x4 Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "8918fe56-d9e1-5eae-97d1-dfc810e443e6" :step/math-academy-id 30053 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162750}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162827 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161966 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161758 :question/id #uuid "cbf8e85c-ffa6-55b9-a087-2a5272f10f25" :question/math-academy-id "e-8049" :question/problem "\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & {\\color{blue}-3} & -1 & -6 \\\\ 7 & {\\color{blue}-2} & -2 & -4 \\\\ -5 & \\bbox[lightgray,2pt]{{\\color{blue}1}} & 4 & 2 \\\\ -8 & {\\color{blue}-3} & -6 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & {\\color{blue}-3} & 11 & 0 \\\\ -3 & {\\color{blue}-2} & 6 & 0 \\\\ {\\color{red}0} & \\bbox[lightgray,2pt]{{\\color{blue}1}} & {\\color{red}0} & {\\color{red}0} \\\\ -23 & {\\color{blue}-3} & 6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $B$ above. By applying three matrix elementary column operations \n\n- $C_1:=\\ldots$\n\n- $C_3:= C_3 - 4C_2$\n\n- $C_4:= \\ldots$\n\neach of the form $C_i:=C_i+kC_2,$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the missing elementary column operations?" :question/worked-solution "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make entries to the left and to the right of $a_{32}={\\color{blue}\\bbox[lightgray,2pt]{1}}$ equal to ${\\color{red}0}.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary column operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -3 & -1 & -6 \\\\ 7 & -2 & -2 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ -8 & -3 & -6 & -4\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}: &={C}_{1}+5{C}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & -3 & -1 & -6 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ -23 & -3 & -6 & -4\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{3}: &={C}_{3}-4{C}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & -3 & 11 & -6 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 6 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -23 & -3 & 6 & -4\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{4}: &={C}_{4}-2{C}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & -3 & 11 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -23 & -3 & 6 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding elementary column operations are\n\n$\\qquad$ $C_1:=C_1+5C_2\\quad$ and $\\quad C_4:=C_4-2C_2.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "958cdcf3-aaba-5275-85f8-9531f10b3d0e" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526480} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162555} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162574} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265942} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265946} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329696} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357509} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357510} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357511} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400492} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402828} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404537} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186639982} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186640003}] :knowledge-point/title "Using Column Operations to Create a Row of Zeros in a 4x4 Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "cdb6468b-12bc-5624-9941-9d18a55ef6f9" :step/math-academy-id 30054} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162828 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161967 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161759 :question/id #uuid "399b7598-c793-5032-9f33-ac02c7b85fe1" :question/math-academy-id "e-8050" :question/problem "\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}\\bbox[lightgray,2pt]{{\\color{blue}1}} & {\\color{blue}4} & {\\color{blue}-7} \\\\ 2 & 2 & -8 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}\\bbox[lightgray,2pt]{{\\color{blue}1}} & {\\color{blue}4} & {\\color{blue}-7} \\\\ {\\color{red}0} & -6 & 6 \\\\ {\\color{red}0} & 10 & -20\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nBy applying elementary row operations of the form $R_i:=R_i+kR_1,$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the elementary row operations?" :question/worked-solution "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make the entries below $a_{11}={\\color{blue}\\bbox[lightgray,2pt]{1}}$ equal to ${\\color{red}0}.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary row operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & -7 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -8 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}: &={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & -7 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 6 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}: &={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & -7 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 10 & -20\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the elementary row operations are\n\n$\\qquad$ $R_2:=R_2-2R_1\\quad$ and $\\quad R_3:=R_3+3R_1.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "908b96db-9463-50b5-89a3-056072c930ba" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049120}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526485} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162553} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162570} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265905} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265908} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357500} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357501} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357502} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400471} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404510} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404517}] :knowledge-point/title "Using Row Operations to Create a Column of Zeros in a 3x3 Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "04f4431c-776f-545b-904c-b0fd1f0a5fab" :step/math-academy-id 30059 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162826}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162829 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161968 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161760 :question/id #uuid "f9912357-1d1d-5413-917e-257dc5dfc0fe" :question/math-academy-id "e-8051" :question/problem "\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -5 & \\bbox[lightgray,2pt]{{\\color{blue}1}} \\\\ 8 & 3 & {\\color{blue}-4} \\\\ 9 & 7 & {\\color{blue}-2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{red}0} & {\\color{red}0} & \\bbox[lightgray,2pt]{{\\color{blue}1}} \\\\ -16 & -17 & {\\color{blue}-4} \\\\ -3 & -3 & {\\color{blue}-2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nBy applying elementary column operations of the form $C_i:=C_i+kC_3,$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the elementary column operations?" :question/worked-solution "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make entries to the left of $a_{13}={\\color{blue}\\bbox[lightgray,2pt]{1}}$ equal to ${\\color{red}0}.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary column operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -5 & 1 \\\\ 8 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 9 & 7 & -2\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}: &={C}_{1}+6{C}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -5 & 1 \\\\ -16 & 3 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 7 & -2\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{2}: &={C}_{2}+5{C}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -16 & -17 & -4 \\\\ -3 & -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the elementary column operations are\n\n$\\qquad$ $C_1:=C_1+6C_3\\quad$ and $\\quad C_2:=C_2+5C_3.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "85e1fe3f-e85e-5ab4-a594-c55b84bb789a" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527289} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162556} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162572} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265938} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265939} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265940} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357506} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357507} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394847} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412026} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186639999}] :knowledge-point/title "Using Column Operations to Create a Row of Zeros in a 3x3 Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "007cc686-090e-50a0-8170-5b1c5eb412f6" :step/math-academy-id 30061 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162827}}] :activity/title "Creating Rows or Columns Containing Zeros Using Gaussian Elimination" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162182}]}
